          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Rock primarily signifies God—and in Christian interpretation Christ—as the firm foundation, refuge, strength, and source of salvation, while some contexts apply the image to Peter, Peter’s confession, stumbling, judgment, or miraculous provision.\",\"book_summary\":\"Rock identifies Jesus Christ specifically as the covenant Head and bedrock of His house and kingdom: the foundation, refuge, and source upon whom His people stand, contrasted with sand.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"God or Christ as the dependable foundation, refuge, and source of salvation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Jesus Christ as the foundation, refuge, and sustaining source of His covenant people and kingdom.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book restricts the symbol to a specifically Christological and covenantal-ecclesial identification. The consensus also permits direct reference to God more generally and recognizes contextual applications such as Peter, Peter’s confession, or a rock of stumbling.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the center: Christ as foundation and refuge is explicitly part of the consensus. The disagreement concerns whether all relevant rock symbolism should be confined to Christ in this covenantal role.\",\"rationale\":\"The same central referent and symbolic force are retained: Christ is the stable foundation, refuge, and source for His people. The added language of covenant Head, house, and kingdom sharpens that established identification rather than replacing it. Because the book presents a narrower Christological extension and does not include the consensus’s broader divine and context-specific applications, the relationship is refined rather than a full match.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The entry may over-systematize a context-dependent image: Deuteronomy 32:4 directly names God as the Rock, while the printed texts do not themselves state that every occurrence denotes Christ specifically as covenant Head. Nevertheless, 1 Corinthians 10:4 explicitly identifies the Rock as Christ, and the other citations strongly support the foundation and refuge elements.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
